1
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:16,000
What would it take to trust a machine
that says it made itself better?

2
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,280
Not a bigger claim — a smaller
one, that can be checked.

3
00:00:21,879 --> 00:00:25,611
Every science begins with
what it refuses to assume.

4
00:00:25,611 --> 00:00:29,701
No agent judges its own claims
— derivation is not truth.

5
00:00:29,701 --> 00:00:34,798
Seeing is not doing — the two coincide
only when confounding is absent,

6
00:00:34,798 --> 00:00:37,884
which is exactly what
may never be assumed.

7
00:00:37,884 --> 00:00:43,557
And improvement only counts when it is
proven, on worlds the prover cannot see.

8
00:00:43,557 --> 00:00:45,280
The rest is engineering.

9
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:49,603
Start by taking everything away.

10
00:00:49,603 --> 00:00:54,954
No names, no context,
no textbook to remember.

11
00:00:54,954 --> 00:01:03,911
What is left is the only thing that
cannot be faked: the ability to find out.

12
00:01:04,513 --> 00:01:10,117
Data can be perfectly
clear, and perfectly wrong.

13
00:01:10,117 --> 00:01:19,156
The only way past a hidden cause is to
reach into the world and move something.

14
00:01:19,156 --> 00:01:22,703
Reading alone fails every time.

15
00:01:22,703 --> 00:01:25,793
Acting succeeds every time.

16
00:01:26,393 --> 00:01:28,959
Real science cuts both ways.

17
00:01:28,959 --> 00:01:34,004
Some guesses survive their
experiment, some die by it —

18
00:01:34,004 --> 00:01:38,224
and the machine finds
out which, the hard way.

19
00:01:38,224 --> 00:01:44,552
It wrote its predictions down first,
so there is no taking them back.

20
00:01:45,153 --> 00:01:55,712
And here is the ghost in the shell —
the model thinking about its own method,

21
00:01:55,712 --> 00:01:58,045
in its own words.

22
00:01:58,045 --> 00:02:02,433
Its idea was good, so it passed.

23
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:09,426
Its next two ideas were
also good. Not good enough.

24
00:02:09,426 --> 00:02:12,718
That is the whole point.

25
00:02:13,318 --> 00:02:17,149
None of this rests on a lucky run.

26
00:02:17,149 --> 00:02:26,274
Hundreds of worlds, thousands of tests —
and any of them can be re-run by anyone,

27
00:02:26,274 --> 00:02:28,413
from a single seed.

28
00:02:28,413 --> 00:02:36,524
And the machine that did it is running
while you watch: on Google Cloud,

29
00:02:36,524 --> 00:02:42,718
answering anyone who asks,
every request on the record.

30
00:02:43,318 --> 00:02:47,747
Spend a quarter of the
budget: same answers.

31
00:02:47,747 --> 00:02:52,575
Spend even less, and the
spread gives you away —

32
00:02:52,575 --> 00:02:58,413
unless the benchmark is rigged
to hide it. Ours was, once.

33
00:02:58,413 --> 00:03:02,639
We found it, fixed it, and published both.

34
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:09,235
In the end we asked the system
the oldest question in science:

35
00:03:09,235 --> 00:03:11,460
do you need repetition?

36
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:16,780
It gave the statistician's
answer — not here, not yet —

37
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:22,680
and told us exactly what kind
of world would change its mind.

38
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:25,723
One gated turn today.

39
00:03:25,723 --> 00:03:28,164
A thousand tomorrow —

40
00:03:28,164 --> 00:03:32,935
and every one of them would still have to

41
00:03:32,935 --> 00:03:35,145
show a receipt. ...

42
00:03:35,145 --> 00:03:36,889
One more thing.

43
00:03:36,889 --> 00:03:42,706
The voice you have been
listening to... is Gemini.

44
00:03:42,706 --> 00:03:48,757
The system just narrated
its own demo. Meta-science.

45
00:03:48,757 --> 00:03:52,711
Free for everyone — human, or A.I.
